Let’s break down **Bubble Sort vs Selection Sort** in C — in **simple terms** with **code and key differences**:

---

## **1. BUBBLE SORT**

### **How it works:**
- Repeatedly compares **adjacent elements**
- **Swaps them** if they are in the wrong order
- Largest element “**bubbles**” to the end

### **Code:**
```c
void bubbleSort(int arr[], int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                // swap
                int temp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
                arr[j + 1] = temp;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

---

## **2. SELECTION SORT**

### **How it works:**
- Selects the **smallest element** in the unsorted part
- Swaps it with the **first unsorted element**
- Repeats for the rest of the array

### **Code:**
```c
void selectionSort(int arr[], int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        int minIndex = i;
        for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
            if (arr[j] < arr[minIndex]) {
                minIndex = j;
            }
        }
        // swap
        int temp = arr[i];
        arr[i] = arr[minIndex];
        arr[minIndex] = temp;
    }
}
```

---

## **3. Key Differences**

| Feature               | **Bubble Sort**                         | **Selection Sort**                        |
|------------------------|------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------|
| Method                | Compares **adjacent** elements           | Finds **min** and swaps                    |
| Swaps per pass        | Many                                     | Only one                                   |
| Efficiency            | Slower for large datasets                | Slightly better for small arrays           |
| Time Complexity       | O(n²)                                    | O(n²)                                      |
| Best Case (sorted)    | O(n) (with optimization)                 | O(n²)                                      |

---

## **Summary:**
- **Bubble Sort** is easy but **inefficient** (too many swaps).
- **Selection Sort** does fewer swaps but is still **O(n²)**.
- For learning: both are good.
- For real projects: use **quicksort** or **merge sort**.

